-- Where an `@nlg` herald may sit relative to a rule's head, and which consumer
-- reads what. smucclaw/l4-ide#972.
--
-- The rule PR #433 landed is that an annotation attaches to what it sits with:
-- trailing a line it describes what is on that line, starting a line of its
-- own it describes what follows -- except under an item of a field list or a
-- GIVEN list, where it describes the item above (rows 15 to 19 below). What
-- that left unsaid is what happens when the head carries an INPUT, because
-- then the last thing the herald sits with may be the input rather than the
-- rule. The appform argument is that input's BINDING occurrence
-- (L4.TypeCheck.ensureNameConsistency does `def` on it, not `mkref`), so the
-- herald lands on the input.
--
-- WHAT THE TWO PROJECTIONS USED TO DO ABOUT THAT, and why it was a defect
-- rather than a placement rule. `l4 render` reads such a herald as the RULE's
-- sentence, on purpose -- L4.Export.Document.decideNlg searches the appform's
-- arguments and its comment says so. `l4 nlg` did not, because its Linearize
-- instance for Resolved sees only the Name a call site resolves to, with no way
-- back up to the enclosing DECIDE. Same bytes, two answers: measured over the
-- nine modules of canon's il/ofek-hadash-2008/encodings/legalese, 65 heralds
-- written, 65 read by `l4 render`, 0 by `l4 nlg`.
--
-- L4.Nlg.promoteHeadInputNlg now MOVES such a herald onto the rule before
-- linearizing, using the same L4.Nlg.decideNlg lookup `l4 render` uses, so
-- every row below reads the same in both projections. It moves rather than
-- copies: left on the input as well, the sentence printed twice in one line at
-- a named-argument call site, once as the heading and again as the input's
-- gloss.
--
-- Measured 2026-09-21 (rows 15 to 19: 2026-10-02, see (d)), one rule per row.
-- Every row but 19 carries BOTH a positional and a `WITH` directive, because
-- the loss used to be per CALL SITE -- a positional call printed the bare name
-- while a `WITH` call printed the sentence as the input's gloss -- and the
-- `.nlg.golden` beside this file is what pins that the two columns now agree.
--
--   #  head shape                      herald placement        nlg positional  nlg WITH   render
--   1  DECIDE `r` IF ...               trailing the head       herald          herald     herald
--   2  DECIDE `r` IF ...               own line above          herald          herald     herald
--   3  `r` MEANS ...                   trailing the head       herald          herald     herald
--   4  `r` MEANS ...                   own line above          herald          herald     herald
--   5  `r` p MEANS ...                 trailing the head       herald          herald     herald
--   6  `r` p MEANS ...                 own line above          herald          herald     herald
--   7  `r` p / herald / MEANS ...      own line BELOW the p    herald          herald     herald
--   8  DECIDE `r` p IS ...             trailing the head       herald          herald     herald
--   9  `r` / herald / p MEANS ...      own line ABOVE the p    herald          herald     herald
--  10  `r` p AKA `a` / herald / MEANS  own line below the AKA  herald          herald     herald
--  11  herald / `r` p AKA `a` MEANS    own line above          herald          herald     herald
--  12  `r` p q / herald / MEANS ...    own line below the q    herald          herald     herald
--  13  herald / `r` p / herald / MEANS both                    OUTER           OUTER      OUTER
--  14  GIVEN p @nlg g / DECIDE `r` IF  trailing the GIVEN name  BARE NAME       gloss      rule's own body
--  15  GIVEN p q / GIVETH / DECIDE     under q, indented        BARE NAME       gloss      rule's own body
--  16  GIVEN p / DECIDE `r` IF         under p, indented        BARE NAME       gloss      rule's own body
--  17  GIVEN p / DECIDE `r` IF         under p, GIVEN's column  herald          herald     herald
--  18  GIVEN p q, both TYPICALLY       under each, indented     BARE NAME       glosses    rule's own body
--  19  section GIVEN p / GIVEN q ...   under p, indented        (no call)       gloss      gloss in body
--
-- Rows 5, 7, 8 and 12 are the repair. Before it they printed the bare rule name
-- positionally; rows 1-4, 6 and 9 already read the herald and are unchanged,
-- which is the point of keeping them here -- the fix must not disturb the
-- placements that already worked.
--
-- Four things the table settles, and none is what you would guess.
--
-- (a) The variable was never `DECIDE` versus `MEANS`, and it was never trailing
-- versus own line. Rows 9 and 6 both put the herald on a line of its own and
-- both always reached the rule; row 9 does it from INSIDE the head, above the
-- input. The condition was which name the herald was written after, and the
-- repair is that it no longer matters.
--
-- (b) Row 13 is why the repair cannot be "always promote". A rule that heralds
-- ITSELF above its head keeps that sentence: L4.Nlg.decideNlg finds the rule's
-- own annotation before it reaches the appform arguments, so nothing is moved
-- and the inner herald stays the input's gloss. An author who writes both meant
-- two different things.
--
-- (c) An `AKA` on the head is a SEPARATE axis (smucclaw/l4-ide#978). Row 10's
-- herald lands on the AKA's NAME, which L4.Nlg.decideNlg searches as another
-- spelling of the head. Row 11's herald lands on the declaration, which the
-- head name does not carry, so a `WITH` call reaches it through the directive
-- splice, which heads a named-argument call with the rule's herald. Both
-- placements read as the rule's sentence everywhere, and a call through the
-- alias name reads the same as one through the head name.
--
-- (d) Rows 15 to 19 are a different question: not which name a herald lands
-- on inside the head, but whether an own-line annotation under the LAST input
-- of a GIVEN list is that input's gloss or the rule's sentence. Ruled
-- 2026-10-02 (Meng): a GIVEN list is a column, like a record's field list, so
-- the annotation under an input describes that input, the last one included,
-- when it is indented further than the GIVEN keyword (rows 15, 16, 18, 19).
-- At the keyword's column or left of it, it describes what follows (row 17).
-- Measured before the change: row 15's gloss was dropped with "Not attached
-- to any valid syntax node", row 16's silently became the rule's sentence,
-- row 18's first gloss landed on the SECOND input while its second was
-- dropped, and row 19's was dropped. Row 19 is a section GIVEN, whose keyword
-- sits at column 5: the tie-break is the keyword's column, not column 1.
--
-- The prelude writes row 5's shape 67 times (`null list @nlg %list% is empty`),
-- so those 67 sentences reach a positional `l4 nlg` call for the first time
-- under the head-input repair. That is the intended effect, not a side effect.

-- Row 1: DECIDE, trailing the head.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `row one` @nlg row one saw %amount%
  IF amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row one` 200
#EVAL `row one` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 2: DECIDE, own line above the head.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row two saw %amount%
DECIDE `row two` IF amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row two` 200
#EVAL `row two` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 3: MEANS, trailing the head.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row three` @nlg row three saw %amount%
  MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row three` 200
#EVAL `row three` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 4: MEANS, own line above the head.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row four saw %amount%
`row four` MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row four` 200
#EVAL `row four` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 5: MEANS with the input in the head, herald trailing. The prelude's own
-- shape, 67 times over. The herald lands on `amount` and is moved to the rule.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row five` amount @nlg row five saw %amount%
  MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row five` 200
#EVAL `row five` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 6: input in the head, herald on its own line ABOVE the head. This is the
-- placement the reference page recommends, and rows 5, 7, 8 and 12 now render
-- identically to it.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row six saw %amount%
`row six` amount MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row six` 200
#EVAL `row six` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 7: the input in the head, herald on a line of its own BELOW it. The shape
-- every one of the 65 heralds in canon's Ofek Hadash encoding uses, and the
-- shape the issue was filed about.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row seven` amount
    @nlg row seven saw %amount%
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row seven` 200
#EVAL `row seven` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 8: the same shape as row 5 under the `DECIDE ... IS` spelling, which is
-- why neither the repair nor this table keys on the keyword.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `row eight` amount @nlg row eight saw %amount%
  IS amount TIMES 0.1

#EVAL `row eight` 200
#EVAL `row eight` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 9: INSIDE the head but ABOVE the input. This is the counterexample to
-- "an annotation inside a head describes the input": the herald is written
-- after the rule's name and before the input, so the rule claimed it all along
-- and nothing is moved here.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row nine`
    @nlg row nine saw %amount%
    amount
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row nine` 200
#EVAL `row nine` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 10: an AKA on the head, herald on a line of its own below it. The AKA's
-- name claims the herald; it is not on an appform argument, so nothing is
-- moved, and it reads as the rule's sentence because the alias is searched as
-- a spelling of the head (smucclaw/l4-ide#978). The third call goes through
-- the alias name.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row ten` amount AKA `row ten alias`
    @nlg row ten saw %amount%
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row ten` 200
#EVAL `row ten` WITH amount IS 200
#EVAL `row ten alias` 200

-- Row 11: the same AKA head with the herald ABOVE it. It lands on the
-- declaration, not on the head name, so `l4 nlg` reaches it through the
-- directive splice: a positional call fills its slots from the arguments, and
-- a `WITH` call takes it as its heading and keeps its `where` clause
-- (smucclaw/l4-ide#978).
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row eleven saw %amount%
`row eleven` amount AKA `row eleven alias` MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row eleven` 200
#EVAL `row eleven` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 12: TWO inputs. The herald attaches to the LAST one, and that is the one
-- moved. Worth pinning, because "the input" is ambiguous in a head that carries
-- several, and because the `where` clause below shows both inputs recovering
-- their own names once the sentence has moved off the second one.
GIVEN floor IS A NUMBER
      amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row twelve` floor amount
    @nlg row twelve saw %amount% over %floor%
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN floor

#EVAL `row twelve` 100 200
#EVAL `row twelve` WITH floor IS 100, amount IS 200

-- Row 13: the rule ALSO heralds itself above its head. The rule's own sentence
-- wins in both projections and the inner herald stays the input's gloss, so an
-- author who writes both still gets both. This is the case that makes the
-- repair conditional rather than unconditional.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row thirteen the rule saw %amount%
`row thirteen` amount
    @nlg row thirteen the input
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row thirteen` 200
#EVAL `row thirteen` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 14: the herald is written on the GIVEN name and the head names NO input.
-- That is a gloss for the input, not a sentence for the rule, and it must read
-- that way: the bare rule name at a positional call, the gloss as the input's
-- label in the `where` clause, and `l4 render` describing the rule by its own
-- body. The type checker hoists GIVEN names into an empty head, so the gloss
-- used to be found as though it sat on a head input: `l4 render` printed
-- "Row fourteen holds if the sum of money.", and after #458 `l4 nlg` printed
-- "the sum of money with 200" (smucclaw/l4-ide#977).
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER @nlg the sum of money
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `row fourteen` IF amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row fourteen` 200
#EVAL `row fourteen` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 15: an own-line gloss under the LAST input, indented past the GIVEN
-- keyword, with a GIVETH next. It describes `amount`, exactly as one under
-- `floor` would describe `floor`.
GIVEN floor  IS A NUMBER
      amount IS A NUMBER
      @nlg the sum of money
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `row fifteen` IF amount GREATER THAN floor

#EVAL `row fifteen` 100 200
#EVAL `row fifteen` WITH floor IS 100, amount IS 200

-- Row 16: the same with no GIVETH, so the slot under the last input is also
-- the slot above the rule. Indented past GIVEN, it is still the input's gloss.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
      @nlg the sum of money
DECIDE `row sixteen` IF amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row sixteen` 200
#EVAL `row sixteen` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 17: the control for row 16. At the GIVEN keyword's column it describes
-- what follows, so it is the rule's sentence, as it always was.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
@nlg row seventeen saw %amount%
DECIDE `row seventeen` IF amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row seventeen` 200
#EVAL `row seventeen` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 18: both inputs carry a TYPICALLY default. The gloss under each is that
-- input's; the default does not stand between an input and the line below it.
GIVEN floor  IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 100
      @nlg the floor
      amount IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 0
      @nlg the sum of money
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `row eighteen` IF amount GREATER THAN floor

#EVAL `row eighteen` 100 200
#EVAL `row eighteen` WITH floor IS 100, amount IS 200

-- Row 19: a section GIVEN, whose keyword is indented under the heading. The
-- gloss is indented past THAT keyword, so it describes `floor`. This row has to
-- be last: a section runs to the end of the file. There is no positional call:
-- `floor` is the section's input, so only a `WITH` call can supply it.
§ `Row nineteen`
    GIVEN floor IS A NUMBER
          @nlg the section's floor

GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `row nineteen` IF amount GREATER THAN floor

#EVAL `row nineteen` WITH floor IS 100, amount IS 200
